          "ruleId": "review.refactor-preserves-restore-checkpoint",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Delta 1411fe2c51..1993d306cf, qwen35_session.rs: the old arms `Err(e) if self.on_gpu() => {fall_back_to_cpu(..)?; Ok(false)}` and `Err(e) => Err(e)` become `Err(e) => self.after_failed_restore(self.on_gpu(), e)`. on_gpu() is still evaluated once, after `restored` is computed (the borrow of backend/checkpoint ends with the match on `restored`), and the helper body is the same fall_back_to_cpu(format!(same message))? ; Ok(false) for GPU and Err(e) for CPU. fall_back_to_cpu clears self.checkpoint, so Ok(false) with the checkpoint dropped is preserved; a fall_back_to_cpu error still propagates via `?`. Behaviour is identical by static reading. Not compiled or run by this reviewer (cargo forbidden on this host)."
          },
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "cited",
            "source": "crates/aprender-serve/src/gguf/inference/forward/qwen35_session.rs:768",
            "excerpt": "Err(e) => self.after_failed_restore(self.on_gpu(), e),",
            "excerpt_sha256": "9a7ea87c6bc21bf28a00105b523ef4dce28477f4f43a697a68435f983f46676c",
            "failure_scenario": "If the helper were given the wrong flag (e.g. evaluated after fall-back, or negated) a GPU restore failure would be returned as Err and abort the turn instead of falling back to CPU; by static reading the flag is self.on_gpu() at the same point as the old guard, so no such change exists.",
            "precision_class": "advisory"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "review.fallback-test-on-flag-not-real-gpu",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "The new test a_failed_restore_falls_back_on_the_device_and_propagates_on_the_cpu can fail on the helper: `f.checkpoint.is_none()` after the on_gpu=true call only holds if fall_back_to_cpu ran (it sets checkpoint = None), the notice assertions need say(fallback_line(..)) with the exact reason text, and the on_gpu=false half asserts the checkpoint and notices are untouched, so swapping the branches fails both halves. Two weaker points: `assert!(!f.on_gpu())` is already true before the call on the synthetic CPU session, so it discriminates nothing; and because the flag is passed in, a mutant of the CALL SITE in restore_checkpoint (`self.on_gpu()` replaced by `true`/`false`) is not killed on a CPU build - only the cuda-gated path would catch it, and no gpu test injects a restore failure."
          },
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "cited",
            "source": "crates/aprender-serve/src/gguf/inference/forward/qwen35_session_tests.rs:800",
            "excerpt": "assert!(f.checkpoint.is_none(), \"the device checkpoint is dropped\");",
            "excerpt_sha256": "c69ac52801a9f1fafe1ed6d4c99002fef80389bffb948512059614b7018b95f3",
            "failure_scenario": "A mutation of the call-site flag in restore_checkpoint (self.on_gpu() -> false) survives on the CPU build: a GPU restore failure would then abort the turn with Err instead of falling back, and no test in this PR would notice.",
            "precision_class": "advisory"
          }
