          "ruleId": "review.obligations-parse-arm-preserved",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Delta 1993d306cf..6beb021e4b, obligations.rs check_contract: old arms were `Err(Load::Parse(_)) if !problems.is_empty() => return problems` and `Err(Load::Parse(why) | Load::Other(why)) => {push; return}`. New: Parse => push `{rel}: {why}` only if problems is empty, then return; Other => push, return. Truth table over (Parse|Other) x (problems empty|non-empty): Parse+non-empty returns problems unchanged (old arm 1, new arm 1); Parse+empty pushes then returns (old arm 2, new arm 1); Other always pushes then returns (old arm 2, new arm 2). Identical in all four cells. The push inside the `if` is unreachable in practice (check_validate reports whenever serde_yaml refuses, and load() parses with the same serde_yaml), which is why the guard-form mutant was equivalent; the new test unparseable_yaml_is_one_problem pins exactly one line and kills a `is_empty -> true` style mutant by count. Read, not run: cargo is forbidden on this host."
          },
          "properties": {
